---
source: tests/snapshots_help_contracts.rs
expression: "help(&scratch, None, &[\"--help\", \"worktree\", \"prune\"])"
---
Usage: worktree prune [--repo PATH] [--dryrun] [--json|--format json] [--verbose] [--remove] [--no-sync]

Global, removal-by-default cleanup of registered worktrees, grouped by canonical directory. Without --repo it enumerates every repository identity the directory registry records (even outside Git) and resolves each through its main checkout, skipping an identity it cannot verify with a bounded reason instead of guessing; --repo PATH restricts the pass to one verified checkout. A directory is removed only when it is a verified non-main, clean directory (the narrow root `.cache` exception is preserved); every associated task is confirmed closed through its own durable task or a proven legacy route; a fresh host occupancy check reports no running or unassociated session in it; and every present idle session the directory's registry associations name is safely relocated to the verified main checkout. A confirmed closed historical task does not keep the directory; a still-open associated task, an unavailable or incomplete occupancy listing, an unresolved session, a mismatched path or session id, a running session, or an unconfirmed move keeps it. A proven absent path has its directory registration and owned associations reclaimed under the operation lock after a fresh row and path check; dry-run reports would-reclaim without writes. An absent main registration is a stale row like any other and is reclaimed the same way, while an existing main checkout stays excluded from the candidate and kept totals. Inaccessible paths and symlinks stay. Existing directories in unverifiable repositories remain protected. Independent histories are preserved; absence requires no provider read or move. The scan reuses one running-session snapshot and destructive boundaries check fresh occupancy. A cache-bearing candidate keeps two fresh checks around the destructive cache deletion: one after the family relocation and before the root .cache is deleted, and one after the deletion and before the worktree removal; a candidate without a root .cache runs the non-mutating cleanliness probe and one final fresh check. In both flows no external Git process sits between the final fresh check and the removal. Kept reason codes distinguish issue state, lookup failure, legacy provenance, local probes and running sessions, and name the session-family, cache, path and registry failures a fresh re-check found. The read-only classification never moves a session; the preview then reads each candidate's fresh occupancy and the lineage the host reports, so it reports the same blockers and the same move eligibility the removal would meet, without moving, deleting, or writing anything. --dryrun (alias --dry-run) is the read-only preview and creates no store, schema, or operation lock. The removal stage is the only place that may move a session. There is no waiting period or age threshold. The local guards — never the main checkout, never a dirty or untracked directory, except the exact root `.cache` exception — are decided before any running-session query, so a main-checkout or dirty directory makes zero host or provider calls. The running-session answer comes from the one connected service; coverage is that service, not a whole-machine multi-service inventory. The default output follows the global --output auto selection: the compact human summary on an interactive terminal and the machine-readable aggregate envelope on a pipe or in CI, while --json or --format json still forces the envelope, --format text forces the summary, and a legacy selection that contradicts an explicit --output is rejected. The summary is one heading, one aggregate count line, one kept-reason counts line, at most five removal-failure examples with their path and reason plus the omitted count, a bounded absent-description line, a skipped-repository count when nonzero, and the measured elapsed time; its size never grows with the number of directories, because it lists no per-directory row. --verbose keeps that summary and adds one bounded row per actual removal, per removal failure, per eligible preview row (never labelled as removed), per absent directory, and per kept directory with its reason; it changes nothing in the machine document. The aggregate envelope is the complete-detail inspection channel. It also carries the measured timings of the pass: the wall clock, the pipeline stages, and the nested closure evaluations and host calls. The reclamation stage times the root .cache clearance or its cleanliness probe, the git worktree removal, the directory registry row removal and the absent registration reclamation, including the calls that fail. The two families overlap, because every nested call is measured inside the stage that made it, so the durations are observations and never an additive total; a closure evaluation counts evaluations, not network requests. The compact text keeps the elapsed line only; --verbose adds the bounded stage and operation breakdown, and the machine document keeps exactly one document either way. --remove is an accepted compatibility no-op and --no-sync is accepted but no longer runs a sync pass: prune never syncs. Branches and registry rows are never deleted by a dry run; a removed directory's registry row is deleted only after the tree is gone. Progress is a human affordance: on an interactive terminal one stderr line is redrawn in place through scanning, verification and deletion, showing completed repositories against the known repository total and cumulative completed directory counts for removal, absence, retention and failure, never a directory path or completion before an action returns, and it is cleared before the result. Orchestrator-only.
